        "original_id": "9b33cf7e-5208-5b73-93e5-2b6ff9b7e305",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Henry Bacon, Abraham Lincoln\n- Places: Washington, D.C.\n- Projects: Lincoln Memorial, Perry Memorial Arch\n- Topics: American national monuments, presidential memorials\n\nFacts:\n- Henry Bacon was an American Beaux-Arts architect born on November 28, 1866, and he designed the Perry Memorial Arch, which was built in 1918.\n- Henry Bacon is best remembered for the Lincoln Memorial in Washington, D.C., which was built from 1915 to 1922 and was his final project.\n- The Lincoln Memorial is an American national monument built to honor Abraham Lincoln, the 16th President of the United States.\n- The Lincoln Memorial was dedicated in 1922 and is one of several monuments built to honor an American president.\n- Henry Bacon died on February 16, 1924.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "f2f88dff-b5de-5fa3-a8d6-08ab9cfbd7c2",
        "text": "Page: Henry Bacon. Sentence: Henry Bacon (November 28, 1866 – February 16, 1924) was an American Beaux-Arts architect who is best remembered for the Lincoln Memorial in Washington, D.C. (built 1915–22), which was his final project.\nPage: Lincoln Memorial. Sentence: The Lincoln Memorial is an American national monument built to honor the 16th President of the United States, Abraham Lincoln.\nPage: Lincoln Memorial. Sentence: Dedicated in 1922, it is one of several monuments built to honor an American president.\nPage: Perry Memorial Arch. Sentence: It was built in 1918 and designed by architect Henry Bacon.",
